// =====================================================================
// CHURCH FATHERS ROUTE  (contest slice, Meta VR Start Developer Competition 2026)
// Backlog V17-V20. Jim's V4 decision (2026-11-06): Socrates asks one question, and
// each answer sends the player to an early church father, who then points them on
// to a contemporary philosopher in the same grove.
//
//   It's not real     -> Gregory of Nyssa (Metaphysics)   -> Michael Heim
//   I don't know      -> Augustine (Epistemology)         -> Nick Bostrom
//   Who cares?        -> John Chrysostom (Values)         -> Robert Nozick
//
// Each father's third answer sends the player to the next grove:
//   Metaphysics -> Epistemology -> Values -> Metaphysics
//
// All lines are paraphrase. No text from Reality+ or from any translation is quoted.
// This file never changes RealityPlus_Map.yarn; Jim adds the one entry line himself.
//
// UNITY HOOKS
//   Contest_Question     Socrates' contest question. Jim: in Map_Start, change the
//                        last line `<<jump Hub_Question>>` to `<<jump Contest_Question>>`.
//                        (Its fourth answer still leads to Hub_Question, so nothing is lost.)
//   Talk_GregoryOfNyssa  Start Node of the Gregory of Nyssa NPC (V18)
//   Talk_Augustine       Start Node of the Augustine NPC (V19)
//   Talk_Chrysostom      Start Node of the John Chrysostom NPC (V20)
//   Slice_End            V26 ending. Jim's Done_Metaphysics, Done_Probability and Done_WellBeing
//                        jump here once per contest route (when $contest_answer is set).
//   Contest_Return       Socrates asks again, after the player walks back to him from Slice_End.
//
// <<handoff NAME>> (YarnWalkCommands, W3) needs the speaker to be a PhilosopherWalker
// (Socrates converted with MIHLab > Convert selected NPC to Walker, task W5) and the
// target to be findable by that name: its Philosopher Id, or a GameObject name that
// contains it. Name the new NPCs "Gregory of Nyssa", "Augustine", "John Chrysostom".
// =====================================================================

title: Contest_Question
position: 6500,1602
tags: hub contest
area: Hub
speaker: Socrates
---
<<declare $came_from = "">>
<<declare $contest_answer = "">>
<<declare $slice_ended = false>>
Socrates: Before I send you anywhere, look at this tree beside us. Bark, leaves, the shade it throws.
Socrates: Now suppose someone built a tree exactly like it inside a computer, and you stood under it wearing a headset.
Socrates: Would that virtual tree be a real object?
-> It's not real.
    <<set $contest_answer to "not_real">>
    Socrates: Not real. Then you think you know what makes a thing real, and the virtual tree lacks it. What is it, I wonder?
    Socrates: A bishop from Cappadocia once asked what a tree is made of, and his answer surprised everyone. Walk with me to Gregory of Nyssa.
    <<handoff "Gregory of Nyssa">>
-> I don't know.
    <<set $contest_answer to "dont_know">>
    Socrates: Good. Saying "I don't know" is where I began too. But can you know anything at all, if you cannot tell a real tree from a made one?
    Socrates: There is a teacher from North Africa who fought that very doubt for years. Walk with me to Augustine.
    <<handoff "Augustine">>
-> Who cares?
    <<set $contest_answer to "who_cares">>
    Socrates: Who cares! That is a real question, though you asked it to dismiss mine. If the shade is cool and the leaves are green, what would be missing?
    Socrates: There is a preacher from Antioch who spent his life asking what is truly worth having. Walk with me to John Chrysostom.
    <<handoff "John Chrysostom">>
-> I'd rather start somewhere else.
    Socrates: Then tell me what troubles you, and we will find the right grove for it.
    <<jump Hub_Question>>
===

title: Talk_GregoryOfNyssa
position: 7000,1602
tags: npc contest
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Gregory
---
<<if $came_from == "Socrates">>
    Gregory: Socrates tells me you think a virtual tree is not real. Then let us find out what a real tree is made of.
<<elseif $came_from == "John Chrysostom">>
    Gregory: My friend John sent you to ask what is real enough to be good. That is my question too. Start with a tree.
<<else>>
    Gregory: Welcome. I am Gregory, bishop of Nyssa in Cappadocia. I spend my days asking what things are made of.
<<endif>>
Gregory: Take away a tree's colour. Then its shape. Then its size, its weight, the way it resists your hand.
Gregory: What is left over, once every quality is gone?
-> Some kind of stuff underneath.
    Gregory: That is what most people say. A hidden stuff that holds the qualities up, like a peg holds a cloak.
    Gregory: But try to think of that stuff without any quality at all. No colour, no shape, no weight. You find nothing to think.
-> Nothing at all.
    Gregory: Yes. I think so too, and it surprised me when I first saw it.
Gregory: Here is my view. A body is not a lump of hidden stuff wearing qualities. It is the qualities themselves, meeting in one place.
Gregory: And each quality is something the mind can grasp. Colour, shape, weight: each is a kind of thought, before it is a thing.
Gregory: So now look again at Socrates' virtual tree. It has colour. It has shape. It stands in its place and you can walk round it.
Gregory: If a tree is a meeting of qualities, why would the virtual one be less real?
-> Because some qualities are missing. You can't lean on it.
    Gregory: Good. Then the question is not whether it is real, but how much of a tree it is. A tree with no weight is a thinner thing.
    Gregory: Michael Heim has thought hard about what virtual things lack, and what they have. Walk with me to him.
    <<handoff "Michael Heim">>
-> It isn't less real. Its qualities are really there.
    Gregory: Careful. You have just argued for something many people would laugh at. I do not laugh.
    Gregory: Michael Heim takes virtual worlds seriously too, in his own way. Walk with me to him, and test the idea on him.
    <<handoff "Michael Heim">>
-> How could I even know which qualities are really there?
    Gregory: Ah. Now you are asking how we know, not what exists. That belongs to the next grove.
    Gregory: Augustine fought the doubters of his day on exactly that ground. Walk with me to him.
    <<handoff "Augustine">>
===

title: Talk_Augustine
position: 7400,1602
tags: npc contest
area: Epistemology
speaker: Augustine
---
<<if $came_from == "Socrates">>
    Augustine: Socrates says you do not know whether the virtual tree is real. Good. I spent years not knowing things like that.
<<elseif $came_from == "Gregory of Nyssa">>
    Augustine: Gregory sent you to ask how you could know which qualities are there. A fair question. Let us start further back.
<<else>>
    Augustine: Welcome. I am Augustine, from North Africa. As a young man the doubters nearly convinced me that nothing can be known.
<<endif>>
Augustine: The doubters said: your senses can deceive you. Dreams feel real. So you can never be sure of anything.
Augustine: Suppose they are right about the tree. Suppose it is a dream, or a machine's picture. Now answer me one thing.
Augustine: While you are being deceived, who is it that is being deceived?
-> I am.
    Augustine: Exactly. If I am deceived, then I exist. Nobody can be fooled who is not there to be fooled.
-> Maybe nobody. Maybe that's an illusion too.
    Augustine: Then who is having the illusion? You cannot doubt without a doubter. The doubt itself proves you are here.
Augustine: So some knowledge survives any doubt. I know that I exist. I know that the tree seems green to me, even if it is not a tree at all.
Augustine: And I know that I want to know the truth. No machine can make that false.
Augustine: So even if all this is a simulation, why does that not leave you with nothing?
-> Because I still know I exist. But not what world I'm in.
    Augustine: Well put. You are certain of yourself and uncertain of the world. Then how should you reason about the world?
    Augustine: Nick Bostrom asks whether we could know we are not simulated. Walk with me to him.
    <<handoff "Nick Bostrom">>
-> Because knowing that much is enough to start reasoning.
    Augustine: Yes. From one sure point you can weigh the rest, even when you cannot be certain of it.
    Augustine: Nick Bostrom weighs the odds that this world is a simulation. Walk with me to him, and check his sums.
    <<handoff "Nick Bostrom">>
-> If I can't know what's real, does it even matter?
    Augustine: Now you have stopped asking what you know and started asking what is worth caring about. That belongs to the next grove.
    Augustine: John Chrysostom preached on that every week in Antioch. Walk with me to him.
    <<handoff "John Chrysostom">>
===

title: Talk_Chrysostom
position: 7800,1602
tags: npc contest
area: WellBeing
speaker: Chrysostom
---
<<if $came_from == "Socrates">>
    Chrysostom: Socrates tells me you asked "Who cares?" about his tree. A good question, if you mean it. I asked it about gold.
<<elseif $came_from == "Augustine">>
    Chrysostom: Augustine sent you to ask whether it matters what is real. It matters more than anything. Let me tell you why.
<<else>>
    Chrysostom: Welcome. I am John, a preacher in Antioch. They call me Chrysostom, "golden mouth", because I never stop talking.
<<endif>>
Chrysostom: In my city the rich wore silk and ate from silver, while beggars lay at their gates. Everyone thought the rich were the lucky ones.
Chrysostom: But think of actors in a theatre. One plays a king and wears a crown. When the play ends, he takes the crown off and goes home poor.
Chrysostom: Wealth, pleasure, a good name: I think these are costumes. They feel like having a good life. They are not the good life.
Chrysostom: So you ask who cares whether the tree is virtual. If it feels the same, what would you be missing?
-> Nothing, if I'm happy.
    Chrysostom: Then you are the man in the crown, sure he is a king. How you feel is not the measure of how you live.
-> The difference between feeling good and being good.
    Chrysostom: Yes. A beggar who is just and patient is richer than the man who feasts. That is not a feeling. It is what he is.
Chrysostom: So I do not ask whether your life feels good. I ask what kind of person it is making you.
Chrysostom: Why should that matter more than how life feels from the inside?
-> Because a pleasant illusion isn't a good life.
    Chrysostom: Then you are ready for a hard question. Robert Nozick asks whether you would plug into a machine of perfect pleasure. Walk with me to him.
    <<handoff "Robert Nozick">>
-> Maybe it doesn't. Maybe feeling good is enough.
    Chrysostom: Many in Antioch agreed with you, and stopped coming to church. Let us test it properly.
    Chrysostom: Robert Nozick built a thought experiment for exactly your view. Walk with me to him, and see if you still hold it.
    <<handoff "Robert Nozick">>
-> Then what is real enough to be good?
    Chrysostom: That is no longer a question about value. It is a question about what exists. That belongs to the next grove.
    Chrysostom: Gregory of Nyssa thinks hard about what things are made of. Walk with me to him.
    <<handoff "Gregory of Nyssa">>
===

title: Slice_End
position: 8200,1602
tags: contest end
area: Hub
---
<<set $slice_ended to true>>
<<if $contest_answer == "not_real">>
    You told Socrates the virtual tree was not real. Gregory of Nyssa asked what any tree is made of, if not the qualities your mind takes in.
<<elseif $contest_answer == "dont_know">>
    You told Socrates you did not know. Augustine showed you the one thing a simulation cannot take from you: you are there to be fooled.
<<else>>
    You asked Socrates who cares. John Chrysostom asked whether a life that only feels good is a good life.
<<endif>>
Socrates' question had three doors: what is real, how we can know it, and why it should matter. You have walked through one of them.
-> Walk me back to Socrates. I want to try another answer.
    <<walk_to Socrates Contest_Return>>
-> I'll stay and look around.
    Then the groves are yours. Every philosopher here has a question waiting.
===

title: Contest_Return
position: 8600,1602
tags: contest hub
area: Hub
speaker: Socrates
---
<<set $slice_ended to false>>
Socrates: Back again! One door opened. Which answer will you test this time?
-> It's not real.
    <<set $contest_answer to "not_real">>
    Socrates: Then ask Gregory of Nyssa what a tree is made of. Walk with me.
    <<handoff "Gregory of Nyssa">>
-> I don't know.
    <<set $contest_answer to "dont_know">>
    Socrates: Then ask Augustine what survives every doubt. Walk with me.
    <<handoff "Augustine">>
-> Who cares?
    <<set $contest_answer to "who_cares">>
    Socrates: Then ask John Chrysostom what is worth having. Walk with me.
    <<handoff "John Chrysostom">>
-> Nothing for now.
    Socrates: Then walk on. The questions will find you.
===
